window.INTERVIEW_EXECUTION["swift-34"] = {"timeBudget": {"navigation": 4, "solution": 9, "coding": 26, "verification": 6}, "navigation": {"say": "All three parts are converging cursors over the same profile, and the whole difficulty is one conditional in each. So the minute I spend up front is on the model: what a post covers, and what a pair of posts is allowed to assume about the ground between them.", "questions": ["For the widest span, do the posts in between matter, or am I asking what two walls could hold regardless of the bed?", "Is the depth wanted per post, or only the total? They are different asks and only one of them shows a whole class of bug.", "Are elevations guaranteed non-negative, and is a reading below datum data or a fault?"], "assumptions": ["Each post covers one interval of channel, which is the standard first-order model these gauges are spaced for.", "The widest span ignores the ground between its two posts: it asks what the walls could hold.", "Elevations are non-negative, and a reading below datum is a miscalibrated post rather than a hollow.", "The depth profile is wanted, not only the total, and the total is a projection of it."], "edgeCases": ["an empty profile and a profile of one post, where no pair exists at all", "a monotone profile, rising or falling, which holds nothing anywhere but still has a widest span", "a flat profile at datum, where every pair holds nothing and the honest answer is that there is no impoundment", "two hollows of exactly the same volume, where the tie rule becomes observable"], "restate": "Given elevations at evenly spaced gauge posts along a channel, report the pair of posts that could impound the most between them, the standing water depth over every post once the channel saturates, the total, and the single largest body of standing water."}, "solution": {"pitch": "Two cursors converging from the ends, three times. What makes this the interesting corner of the family is that there is no target to compare a sum against, so the rule for which cursor moves has to be argued from a discard: whatever I advance, I must be able to say that everything it walks past was already proven no better. For the widest span that argument is about the shorter wall capping the capacity. For the depths it is about the shorter side's water level already being settled. The third part is not a new sweep at all, it reads the depths the second part produced.", "dataStructures": ["Two integer cursors and, for the depth pass, two running maxima. Nothing that grows with the profile except the answer itself.", "One array of depths, which is the output rather than working state: the pass fills each slot exactly once and never reads one back.", "A record type for the impoundment carrying both post ids as well as the number, so a planner can go and look at the posts.", "A record type for the pond carrying inclusive bounds and a volume, derived by a plain scan of the depths."], "types": ["Posts are a struct with an id rather than bare integers, because every answer here is something a person has to go and stand next to.", "The impoundment and the pond are separate types rather than one span type, because they answer different questions and share only their shape.", "The depth of a post is a plain integer array indexed alongside the profile, which is the honest representation of one number per post.", "The error names the post rather than the index, because an index means nothing to whoever has to recalibrate the gauge."], "composition": "The total is the sum of the depth profile and nothing else, which is the composition made literal: the richer method is the primitive and the simpler one is its projection, so there is exactly one converging depth pass in the file. The largest pond reads that same array and never re-derives a depth. The widest span stands alone because its running state is a best pair rather than a pair of running maxima, and rather than force a call I would rather have the suite assert the relationship that does hold: the widest span's capacity is never less than the total water, because the rectangle it describes contains all of it.", "tradeoff": "Returning the depths costs an array where the total alone would cost two integers. I want that array anyway, because the total is exactly the number that can stay right while the profile underneath it is wrong, and if this ran over a profile too large to hold I would offer a streaming total as a second method rather than give up the profile.", "trap": "One conditional decides the whole thing, and the way to get it wrong is to advance the taller side. It is the discard argument run backwards, and on a profile that leans it invents water over ground that would drain. The second trap is ordering inside the branch: raise the running maximum, then record the depth. Recording first reports a negative depth at every post that is itself the tallest so far."}, "coding": {"order": ["Write the validation first, since all four entry points need it and it is three lines.", "Write the widest span next, saying the discard argument out loud before writing the conditional that follows from it.", "Check it against a profile that leans, where advancing the wrong side gives a visibly smaller answer.", "Write the depth pass, and write the running-maximum update above the depth line deliberately rather than by accident.", "Make the total a sum of that array immediately, so there is never a second walk to keep in step.", "Write the largest pond last as a plain scan of the depths, with the tie rule stated in a comment."], "commentary": ["There is no target to compare against here, so the move rule has to come from a discard argument. The capacity is capped by the shorter wall, so moving the taller one can only shorten the span.", "That is why the shorter side advances: every pair it walks past is one I have already shown to be no better than the pair I just measured.", "In the depth pass each cursor carries the tallest post it has walked past. Whichever post is shorter is the side whose water level is already settled.", "I raise the running maximum first and record the depth second. The other order reports a negative depth at every post that is itself the tallest so far.", "The cursors run until they pass each other, not until they meet, or the last post never gets a depth.", "The total is the sum of that array. A total that walks the profile again is the same idea written twice, and the second copy is where they drift apart.", "The largest pond reads the depths rather than deriving anything. A maximal run of positive depths is exactly what a body of standing water is.", "I replace the best run only on a strictly greater volume, so two equal ponds resolve to the earlier one rather than to whichever the scan reached last."], "checkIn": "Before the depth pass, say: I am going to return the depth at every post and make the total a sum of it, rather than returning the total alone. It costs an array, and it buys a whole class of bug being visible. Does that trade sound right for what you want to see?"}, "verification": {"walkthroughs": ["Walk the worked twelve-post profile through the depth pass: the depths come out as zero, zero, one, zero, one, two, one, zero, zero, one, zero, zero, and the total is six.", "Walk the same profile through the widest span: the two posts of height two, seven intervals apart, give fourteen, and no taller pair beats it because the taller pairs are closer together.", "Walk a three-post profile whose first post is the tallest: the middle post holds one, and advancing the taller side would claim two.", "Walk a basin of one deep hollow between two banks and confirm it fills to the level of the lower bank rather than the higher one.", "Walk a profile of one post through the depth pass and confirm the answer is a single zero, which is what recording before raising the running maximum gets wrong."], "tests": "Run the analyzer's suite and read the three parts in order. The leaning three-post fixture appears in both of the first two suites and is the one that catches advancing the wrong side. The single-post fixture is what catches the ordering inside the branch. The suite also checks the sweep against looking at every pair, and checks that the widest span's capacity is never less than the total water, which is the cross-part relationship this problem can honestly assert.", "complexity": "Every part is a single pass, linear in the number of posts. The depth pass uses one array for its output and two integers of working state, where the prefix-maximum formulation would use two arrays of working state as well; that is the whole reason the converging form is worth knowing. The largest pond is another linear scan over the array already produced.", "moreTime": "Uneven post spacing, which turns every width into a distance and is a good test of whether the model was understood or memorised. A permeable bed, where each post drains at some rate and the question becomes how long the water stands. And streaming the profile, where the total survives and the depth array does not, which is exactly the trade this design made in the other direction."}, "code": "public struct GaugePost: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let id: String\n    public let elevation: Int\n\n    public init(id: String, elevation: Int) {\n        self.id = id\n        self.elevation = elevation\n    }\n}\n\npublic struct Impoundment: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let leftID: String\n    public let rightID: String\n    public let capacity: Int\n\n    public init(leftID: String, rightID: String, capacity: Int) {\n        self.leftID = leftID\n        self.rightID = rightID\n        self.capacity = capacity\n    }\n}\n\npublic struct Pond: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let startIndex: Int\n    public let endIndex: Int\n    public let volume: Int\n\n    public init(startIndex: Int, endIndex: Int, volume: Int) {\n        self.startIndex = startIndex\n        self.endIndex = endIndex\n        self.volume = volume\n    }\n}\n\npublic enum ProfileError: Error, Equatable, Sendable {\n    case negativeElevation(id: String)\n    case notImplemented\n}\n\npublic struct FloodplainAnalyzer: Sendable {\n    public init() {}\n\n    // MARK: Part 1 - Widest impoundable span\n\n    /// The pair of posts that could hold back the most water between them,\n    /// measured as the shorter of the two elevations times the number of\n    /// intervals separating them.\n    ///\n    /// Two cursors converge from the ends. There is no target to compare a sum\n    /// against here, so the move rule comes from a discard argument instead:\n    /// the span's capacity is capped by the shorter side, and moving the taller\n    /// side inward can only shorten the span without ever raising that cap.\n    /// So the shorter side is the one that moves, and every pair it discards\n    /// was already proven no better than the pair just measured.\n    public func widestImpoundment(profile: [GaugePost]) throws(ProfileError) -> Impoundment? {\n        try validate(profile)\n        guard profile.count >= 2 else { return nil }\n\n        var lo = 0\n        var hi = profile.count - 1\n        var best: Impoundment?\n\n        while lo < hi {\n            let capacity = min(profile[lo].elevation, profile[hi].elevation) * (hi - lo)\n            if capacity > (best?.capacity ?? 0) {\n                best = Impoundment(leftID: profile[lo].id, rightID: profile[hi].id, capacity: capacity)\n            }\n            if profile[lo].elevation <= profile[hi].elevation {\n                lo += 1\n            } else {\n                hi -= 1\n            }\n        }\n\n        return best\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 2 - Standing depth at every post\n\n    /// The depth of standing water above every post once the channel has\n    /// saturated.\n    ///\n    /// The same two cursors, now each carrying the tallest post it has walked\n    /// past. Whichever side is shorter is the side whose answer is already\n    /// settled: nothing beyond the other cursor can be lower than the side\n    /// standing opposite it, so the running maximum on the shorter side is the\n    /// water level over that post, whatever the rest of the profile does.\n    ///\n    /// The running maximum is updated before the depth is recorded. Recording\n    /// first reports a negative depth at every post that is itself the tallest\n    /// so far, which is why the profile is worth exposing: a total can stay\n    /// plausible while the profile underneath it is wrong.\n    public func pondingProfile(profile: [GaugePost]) throws(ProfileError) -> [Int] {\n        try validate(profile)\n        guard !profile.isEmpty else { return [] }\n\n        var depths = [Int](repeating: 0, count: profile.count)\n        var lo = 0\n        var hi = profile.count - 1\n        var leftMax = 0\n        var rightMax = 0\n\n        while lo <= hi {\n            // The shorter side is the one that moves. Advancing the taller\n            // side is the discard argument run backwards, and it invents\n            // water over ground that would drain.\n            if profile[lo].elevation <= profile[hi].elevation {\n                leftMax = max(leftMax, profile[lo].elevation)\n                depths[lo] = leftMax - profile[lo].elevation\n                lo += 1\n            } else {\n                rightMax = max(rightMax, profile[hi].elevation)\n                depths[hi] = rightMax - profile[hi].elevation\n                hi -= 1\n            }\n        }\n\n        return depths\n    }\n\n    /// The total standing water across the whole profile.\n    ///\n    /// This is the sum of the depth at every post. There is one converging walk\n    /// in this file and the total is a projection of it, because a total that\n    /// derives itself separately is a second implementation of the same idea\n    /// with none of the evidence.\n    public func totalPondedVolume(profile: [GaugePost]) throws(ProfileError) -> Int {\n        try pondingProfile(profile: profile).reduce(0, +)\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 3 - The largest single pond\n\n    /// The single body of standing water holding the most, as the maximal run\n    /// of consecutive posts with water above them.\n    ///\n    /// This reads the depths rather than re-deriving them. Where two ponds hold\n    /// the same volume the earlier one wins, so the answer never depends on\n    /// which way the scan happened to run.\n    public func largestPond(profile: [GaugePost]) throws(ProfileError) -> Pond? {\n        let depths = try pondingProfile(profile: profile)\n\n        var best: Pond?\n        var index = 0\n\n        while index < depths.count {\n            guard depths[index] > 0 else {\n                index += 1\n                continue\n            }\n            var end = index\n            var volume = 0\n            while end < depths.count, depths[end] > 0 {\n                volume += depths[end]\n                end += 1\n            }\n            if volume > (best?.volume ?? 0) {\n                best = Pond(startIndex: index, endIndex: end - 1, volume: volume)\n            }\n            index = end\n        }\n\n        return best\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Shared validation\n\n    /// A gauge below datum is a miscalibrated post rather than a hollow, and\n    /// the report names the post so someone can go and look at it.\n    private func validate(_ profile: [GaugePost]) throws(ProfileError) {\n        for post in profile where post.elevation < 0 {\n            throw .negativeElevation(id: post.id)\n        }\n    }\n}\n"};
